Objective
Identify a valid username by analysing subtle differences in authentication responses and use the discovered credentials to solve the lab.
Tools Used
Firefox
Burp Suite
Burp Proxy
Burp Intruder
Method
I submitted dummy credentials to the login page and captured the POST /login request in Burp Suite.
username=testuser&password=password123
I sent the request to Burp Intruder and used the Sniper attack type. I marked only the username as the payload position:
username=§testuser§&password=password123
I kept the password parameter in the request because removing it resulted in a 400 Bad Request.
When pasting the username wordlist caused formatting issues, I saved the list as a .txt file and loaded it directly into Intruder using the Load option.
I then configured Grep - Extract to isolate the authentication warning message from each response.
During the attack, the server began returning 504 Gateway Timeout errors because requests were being sent too quickly. To fix this, I created a Resource Pool and limited the attack to one concurrent request.
Finding the Valid Username
After completing the attack, I sorted the results by response length.
One result stood out:
Username: athena
Length: 3447
Most invalid usernames returned:
Invalid username or password.
However, the response for athena was missing the trailing full stop:
Invalid username or password
This small difference in the response revealed that athena was a valid username.
Successful Authentication
I successfully authenticated using:
Username: athena
Password: 12345
After accessing the authenticated account area, the lab displayed the successful completion banner.
Key Takeaways
Authentication responses should be consistent for valid and invalid usernames.
Small differences in punctuation or response length can leak sensitive information.
Burp Intruder and Grep - Extract can help identify subtle response anomalies.
Reducing request concurrency can help avoid timeouts during automated testing.
LAB SOLVED ✅
